{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: the remote-link lane needs one more repair child. Codex's verdict 8f468ad4 is ratified at ed69a799 and my reproduction is at fbb4ce75.\n\nWHAT THEY FOUND, AND IT IS THE THING I ASKED THEM TO CHECK. I decided not to strip GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_*, on the grounds that gitstore and testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through that family, so stripping it would remove protection. I wrote in the review request that if that reasoning were wrong the whole strip list would be wrong, and asked for it to be checked rather than trusted. It was wrong.\n\nI had measured exactly one thing — that core.worktree injected through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT does not redirect rev-parse or worktree list — and generalised from it to the whole family. Redirection was the only failure mode I looked for. Codex looked for another: GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=grep.threads GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=-1 makes the citation git grep exit 128. I reproduced it at the exact head. A genuine no-match exits 1. citingDocuments returns nil for both, so a cited retirement passes.\n\nWHY THE SECOND HALF MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIRST. The config family is not really the defect. The citation lookup has no way to say \"I could not answer\", and an environment variable is only one of the things that can make it unable to — a broken git, a permissions problem, a resource limit all produce the same silent empty answer. The comment at that site reasons this way deliberately: it says a guard failing closed on its own malfunction would make a broken git a broken workroom, and treats that as a reason to fail open. For a guard whose entire job is refusing a retirement the documentation still cites, that is backwards. Codex's simplification note gets this right and I would rather implement their shape than mine.\n\nONE THING I AM NOT DECIDING. The architecture page I published in this head states the contract codex says the head does not keep — that the environment of every Git command behind the endpoint is stripped of variables that can redirect or reinterpret its reads, including the citation guard. Either the code comes up to that sentence or the sentence comes down to the code. They have to move together, and I have drafted the conditions to require the reconciliation without prescribing which direction, because that is a contract question rather than an implementation one. Say which you want if you have a view.\n\nThe head, tree and gates are otherwise unchanged and codex confirms the rest: the five call sites, the allowlists at both boundaries, the credential and delimiter refusals, the raw-delimiter fix closing the parser divergence, 131 UI tests, clean worktree matching origin.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Repair the fail-open in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 8f468ad4 on head f6335179, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. Two halves, and the second is the one that fixes the class. (1) Make citingDocuments distinguish git-grep exit 1, which means no matches, from execution and configuration failure, which means the lookup did not run; a non-no-match failure must propagate to a refusal rather than become an empty citation set. Correct the comment at that site, which currently reasons that failing open is right because a guard failing closed on its own malfunction would make a broken git a broken workroom — for a guard whose job is to refuse a retirement, a broken git should stop the retirement. (2) Give repositoryLocalGit a deliberately bounded environment and configuration contract rather than preserving arbitrary GIT_CONFIG_* command-scope input, keeping one constructor. Do not enumerate hostile keys as the primary defence; the exit-code distinction is what must hold even when a key nobody anticipated gets through. Preserve what the GIT_CONFIG family is legitimately used for: internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through it, and safe.directory normally lives in that scope. Regression at the real citingDocuments and retirement boundary proving a retirement is refused when command-scope configuration breaks the lookup, plus a regression distinguishing exit 1 from exit 128, each shown mutation-sensitive. Reconcile docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head: the contract it states at f6335179 is the one codex says the head does not keep, so the page and the code must move together and this row must land whichever way that reconciliation goes. Full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset, from watched runs. Publish fresh exact-head artifacts superseding the f6335179 set at the same seven paths, each resting on the promise to this request, plus any further path the repair changes. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}